#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171756 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb8fa39c-a982-569a-b2e0-833c51259711" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 3 or 9"}] :question/id #uuid "b926f4d4-513a-5b05-8ce2-bedc39784ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-15012" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $3$ and $9$?\n\n1. $176\\,850$\n2. $1\\,362\\,448$\n3. $68\\,543\\,031$" :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3$. Likewise, a number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9$.\n\n- The sum of the digits of $176\\,850$ is\n$1+7+6+8+5+0 = 27$,\nwhich is divisible by both $3$ and $9$. So, $176\\,850$ is divisible by both $3$ and $9$.\n- The sum of the digits of $1\\,362\\,448$ is\n$1+3+6+2+4+4+8 = 28$,\nwhich is *not* divisible by $3$. So, $1\\,362\\,448$ is *not* divisible by $3$. Also, since it is not divisible by $3$, it cannot be divisible by $9$ either.\n- The sum of the digits of $68\\,543\\,031$ is\n$6+8+5+4+3+0+3+1 = 30$,\nwhich is divisible by $3$, but not divisible $9$. So, $68\\,543\\,032$ is divisible by $3$, but it is not divisible by $9$.\n\nTherefore, only I is divisible by $3$ and $9$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171757 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171933 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b483f9f7-47a9-5725-b1f1-fe0ea39a7e96" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 6"}] :question/id #uuid "2d40d35a-54e1-5d01-9e0f-345a290336ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-15013" :question/problem "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $93\\,455\\,13\\square$ divisible by $6$?\n\n1. $3$\n2. $6$\n3. $8$" :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3$. Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3$, we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3$.\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n9+3+4+5+5+1+3+\\square = 30+\\square\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{3}}$, which is not divisible by $2$. Therefore, it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{6}}$, which is divisible by $2$. The sum of the digits is $30 + {\\color{blue}{6}} = 36$, which is divisible by $3$, so the number is also divisible by $3$. Therefore, the number is divisible by $6$.\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{8}}$, which is divisible by $2$. However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $30+{\\color{blue}{8}}=38$, which is not divisible by $3$. So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171758 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb0ae539-5348-5a2c-b226-c009ce6d6094" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 7"}] :question/id #uuid "dadf214d-16d0-5dbb-aa89-89f4f8e7c79f" :question/math-academy-id "e-15014" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7$?\n\n1. $637$\n2. $368$\n3. $112$" :question/worked-solution "To check if a number is divisible by $7$, double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7$.\n\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{63}}{\\color{red}{7}}$, we have that\n${\\color{blue}{63}}-2({\\color{red}{7}})=63-14=49$\nwhich is divisible by $7$. Therefore, $637$ is divisible by $7$.\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{36}}{\\color{red}{8}}$, we have that\n${\\color{blue}{36}}-2({\\color{red}{8}})=36-16=20$\nwhich is not divisible by $7$. Therefore, $368$ is not divisible by $7$.\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{2}}$, we have that\n${\\color{blue}{11}}-2({\\color{red}{2}})=11-4=7$\nwhich is divisible by $7$. Therefore, $112$ is divisible by $7$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb8fa39c-a982-569a-b2e0-833c51259711" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 3 or 9"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d7da50-1e3d-5265-b31c-a7df48b1aaf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171238} {:answer/id #uuid "8bce3782-9948-5220-a5f8-12960a5f3023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171239} {:answer/id #uuid "c956f7f1-d1f2-5b1d-8df4-31643ebcd9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171240} {:answer/id #uuid "084d54cd-561f-5473-b3b9-1cecf0460963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171241} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbb9f4f-57d7-577a-b320-a0abad262013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171242}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9a78fc-7618-5992-89a0-afd4290ad38c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171237}] :question/id #uuid "335dd6d1-31fa-5c3c-b1bf-3369de1a0d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-85143" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $3$?\n\n1. $187349$\n2. $3121557$\n3. $99125041$"}]}
